How to measure the time of a signal crossing a threshold.

We set up a toy example. The symbol t is time [s] and f(t) = sin(time/s)V.
So, we have f(0) = 0, and f(1s) about x=.8414709848078965V. We want to find the
argument of f where it crosses x, which we already know it 1s.

In this example, we set up a sine shaped voltage source and simulate a
transient.

== Post processing approach

We can simulate a sine wave, record it and then find the crossing in the
data. Finding such crossings can be done with the "measure" command.

$ gnucap cross_meas.gc
[ various times related to the crossing ]

See comments in cross_meas.gc. TL;DR; it does not produce satisfying figures.
Don't expect this to work reliably in more complicated circuits.

== Event approach

Now use a "cross event". A cross event is an event at a specific time related
to a signal crossing. Here it is set to be the time when the signal crosses
the threshold from above, x = sin(1.).

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc cross_evt.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o cross_evt.so
$ gnucap cross_evt.gc
[ crossing reported at about 1. + 2.5e-09 ]

== Higher order post processing

This is an exercise. Add an option to "measure .. cross" to select the
interpolation order ("measure .. at" already has it"). Compute a better
crossing time. Quantify the improvement, consider the added complexity.
